    "headline": "A serious face may be one with nervous, blinking eyes — and the kanji only fake the logic.",
    "morphemes": [],
    "sources": [
      {
        "quote": "Appears to be root maji, which is cognate with まじまじ (majimaji, “staringly”), 瞬ぐ (majirogu, “to wink”) + 目 (me, “eye; visually prominent feature”), from the sense of eyes blinking nervously when having a straight face. First attested in the Nise Monogatari, around 1639–1640."
      },
      {
        "quote": "The kanji spelling is an example of jukujikun (熟(じゅく)字(じ)訓(くん)) mixed with ateji (当(あ)て字(じ)), from 真 (ma-, “real”) + 面 (“face”) + 目 (me, “eye”). Compare English true colours."
      }
    ],
    "examples": [
      {
        "id": 103701,
        "ja": "彼は真面目な性格だ。",
        "en": "He is of a serious turn of mind.",
        "enId": 299991,
        "form": "真面目な",
        "furigana": [
          ["彼", "かれ"],
          ["は"],
          ["真面目", "まじめ"],
          ["な"],
          ["性格", "せいかく"],
          ["だ。"]
        ]
      },
      {
